WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.629
The most common failure is trusting TypeScript types for runtime data.

2
00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:12.885
A request body annotated as AdminUpdateRequest is still whatever the client sent until runtime validation proves otherwise.

3
00:00:12.885 --> 00:00:21.681
Unsafe any and broad type assertions make this worse by removing compiler pressure at the exact places where discipline is needed most.

4
00:00:21.681 --> 00:00:32.868
During review, any use of any, unknown-to-specific casts, or large assertions near an input boundary should raise a simple question: where was this data actually checked.

5
00:00:32.868 --> 00:00:35.877
Mass assignment is another frequent problem.

6
00:00:35.877 --> 00:00:43.207
It occurs when user-controlled fields are copied into an object that has properties the user should not control.

7
00:00:43.207 --> 00:00:50.459
In TypeScript, this can look deceptively clean because object spread and partial update types are convenient.

8
00:00:50.459 --> 00:01:05.582
Validation should reject unexpected fields, map allowed fields intentionally, and keep server-controlled properties such as role, ownerId, tenantId, approval status, and audit fields out of user-controlled assignment paths.

9
00:01:05.582 --> 00:01:15.457
Prototype pollution, path traversal, command injection risks, and unsafe deserialization patterns all become more likely when validation is vague.

10
00:01:15.457 --> 00:01:22.633
At a high level, prototype pollution involves unsafe object handling that can influence inherited behavior.

11
00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:30.580
Path traversal involves treating user input as part of a filesystem path without strict constraints and safe resolution.

12
00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:37.369
Command injection risk appears when unchecked strings influence shell commands or process execution.

13
00:01:37.369 --> 00:01:45.548
Defensive validation does not make dangerous APIs safe by magic, but it narrows the inputs and supports safer design choices.

14
00:01:45.548 --> 00:01:49.097
Frontend and backend validation gaps are also common.

15
00:01:49.097 --> 00:01:56.890
A React form may prevent invalid input in normal use, but an attacker or tester can send requests directly to the backend.

16
00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:00.207
A mobile client may lag behind the server contract.

17
00:02:00.207 --> 00:02:03.988
A partner integration may omit fields after a deployment.

18
00:02:03.988 --> 00:02:06.997
The backend should validate as the authority.

19
00:02:06.997 --> 00:02:09.929
The frontend should validate for usability.

20
00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:18.184
Treating those as separate responsibilities prevents a friendly user experience from being mistaken for a security control.
